CORE
main.cpp
--cpp11
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ one-parameter constructor template: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ three-parameter constructor template: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
no body for callee
--
Two constructor templates of a partial specialization, both defined out of
line.  The instantiated one-parameter template took no definition: among
same-named definitions the one with the most template parameters was
preferred before the arity was compared, and that was the three-parameter
template's.  libc++'s std::function defines `template <class _Fp,
class> function(_Fp)' and its allocator-extended constructor templates,
which have more template parameters, out of line.
